ke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gosanke</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gosanke, the 'three honourable houses', were the three senior branch families of the Tokugawa, founded by sons of Tokugawa Ieyasu: the Owari, Kii and Mito houses.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/goseong-county-south-gyeongsang"><span class="enc-name">Goseong County, South Gyeongsang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Goseong is a county on the south coast of South Gyeongsang Province, South Korea, facing a deeply indented bay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gospel-of-john.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gospel of John</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gospel of John is the fourth of the canonical gospels of the New Testament, probably written around 90–110 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gospel-of-luke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gospel of Luke</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gospel of Luke is the third of the four canonical gospels of the New Testament, written in Greek probably around 80–90 CE by the same author as the Acts of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospels"><span class="enc-name">Gospels</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gospels are the four accounts of the life, teaching, death and resurrection of Jesus in the New Testament, attributed to Matthew, Mark, Luke and John.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gostynin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclope